1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,432
This is the NUCTECH™ MW1000AA, a millimeter-wave body inspection device.

2
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:12,459
In the next few minutes we'll walk through its whole ecosystem: why it exists, what it is,

3
00:00:12,459 --> 00:00:18,495
how it works, who is involved, where and when it's used, and the value chain and know-how behind it.

4
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:24,296
By the end, you should understand this product end to end: from physics, to passengers, to procurement.

5
00:00:25,946 --> 00:00:27,325
Start with the problem.

6
00:00:27,325 --> 00:00:31,223
Every checkpoint, at an airport, a border, a prison or a stadium,

7
00:00:31,223 --> 00:00:37,610
must answer one question quickly: is this person carrying something dangerous under their clothing?

8
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:42,557
The classic walk-through metal detector is fast and cheap, but it only senses metal.

9
00:00:42,557 --> 00:00:48,013
Ceramic knives, plastic explosives, liquids, powders and drugs walk straight through.

10
00:00:48,013 --> 00:00:51,548
A manual pat-down catches more, but it is slow, intrusive,

11
00:00:51,548 --> 00:00:55,777
inconsistent between officers, and uncomfortable for everyone.

12
00:00:55,777 --> 00:00:59,104
Older X-ray backscatter body scanners could see more,

13
00:00:59,104 --> 00:01:06,145
but they used ionizing radiation and produced revealing images, which raised health and privacy concerns.

14
00:01:06,145 --> 00:01:10,669
So operators need screening that is safe, non-contact, automatic,

15
00:01:10,669 --> 00:01:15,263
consistent, fast enough for peak hours, and respectful of privacy.

16
00:01:15,263 --> 00:01:21,321
That gap is exactly where active millimeter-wave screening, and the MW1000AA, fit.

17
00:01:22,971 --> 00:01:25,122
Here is the product.

18
00:01:25,122 --> 00:01:36,541
The MW1000AA is a walk-in portal: about 1.4 m wide, 1.2 m deep and 2.5 m tall, weighing around 550 kg.

19
00:01:36,541 --> 00:01:39,664
The passenger stands in a channel 750 mm wide.

20
00:01:39,664 --> 00:01:43,533
The side panels house the millimeter-wave imaging system.

21
00:01:43,533 --> 00:01:51,519
A 15-inch color touch screen gives the operator a simple interface, and LED indicators show status at a glance.

22
00:01:51,519 --> 00:01:57,301
An optional foot metal detector in the floor checks shoes, so passengers may not need to take them off.

23
00:01:57,301 --> 00:02:05,285
The key numbers: a 2-second scan, about 4 seconds per inspection, 1 kW of power, and under 60 dB of noise.

24
00:02:05,285 --> 00:02:08,898
It runs on standard 110 or 220 volt mains.

25
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:14,051
It detects metallic and non-metallic weapons, IEDs, liquids, gels and drugs,

26
00:02:14,051 --> 00:02:20,017
flags them automatically, and shows results on a cartoon figure instead of a body image.

27
00:02:20,017 --> 00:02:26,197
It is certified to ECAC security scanner Standard 2.1 Type A, and carries the CE mark.

28
00:02:27,847 --> 00:02:31,342
So how does it see through clothing? It starts with physics.

29
00:02:31,342 --> 00:02:35,944
Millimeter waves are radio waves with wavelengths between 1 and 10 millimeters,

30
00:02:35,944 --> 00:02:38,783
the band between microwaves and infrared.

31
00:02:38,783 --> 00:02:43,511
They are non-ionizing: they don't carry enough energy to alter atoms or DNA.

32
00:02:43,511 --> 00:02:49,995
And active scanners like this one transmit at very low power, typically far below a mobile phone.

33
00:02:49,995 --> 00:02:53,177
Clothing fabric is almost transparent to these waves.

34
00:02:53,177 --> 00:02:56,179
Human skin, full of water, reflects them strongly.

35
00:02:56,179 --> 00:02:59,662
A hidden object, whether metal, plastic, liquid or powder,

36
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:04,734
reflects differently from skin, and that contrast is what the system detects.

37
00:03:04,734 --> 00:03:08,516
It's called active because the device creates its own signal.

38
00:03:08,516 --> 00:03:13,723
Antenna arrays in the side panels transmit and receive across a wide frequency band,

39
00:03:13,723 --> 00:03:18,760
sampling the body from many positions, front and back, in about 2 seconds.

40
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:25,110
Wide bandwidth gives depth resolution, and the many antenna positions give height and width resolution.

41
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:28,563
A reconstruction algorithm then combines all the echoes,

42
00:03:28,563 --> 00:03:33,760
a form of holography, into a 3D image of the body surface and anything on it.

43
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:35,567
Next comes the intelligence.

44
00:03:35,567 --> 00:03:40,474
A neural network, trained with deep learning on large sets of labeled scans,

45
00:03:40,474 --> 00:03:43,960
looks for anomalies that don't belong on a human body.

46
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:47,315
This is Automatic Threat Recognition, or ATR.

47
00:03:47,315 --> 00:03:48,427
Finally, privacy.

48
00:03:48,427 --> 00:03:51,372
The operator never needs to see a body image.

49
00:03:51,372 --> 00:03:55,167
Any suspicious area is marked on a generic cartoon figure.

50
00:03:55,167 --> 00:03:59,967
No alarm means a green light; an alarm shows exactly where to check.

51
00:04:01,617 --> 00:04:02,496
Now, the people.

52
00:04:02,496 --> 00:04:06,624
Many stakeholders touch this product, and each has different needs.

53
00:04:06,624 --> 00:04:11,591
At the center is the passenger, who wants to be safe, fast, and treated with dignity.

54
00:04:11,591 --> 00:04:14,863
Next is the screening officer, who guides the passenger,

55
00:04:14,863 --> 00:04:19,403
reads the result, and does a targeted pat-down when there is an alarm.

56
00:04:19,403 --> 00:04:23,236
Above them, the security supervisor and checkpoint manager plan lanes,

57
00:04:23,236 --> 00:04:26,806
staffing and throughput, and own the performance numbers.

58
00:04:26,806 --> 00:04:32,217
The checkpoint owner, such as an airport operator, customs agency, rail or metro operator,

59
00:04:32,217 --> 00:04:36,996
prison service or building owner, buys the equipment and pays to run it.

60
00:04:36,996 --> 00:04:42,398
Regulators and certifiers, such as ECAC in Europe and national civil aviation authorities,

61
00:04:42,398 --> 00:04:45,159
set detection standards and approve equipment.

62
00:04:45,159 --> 00:04:48,610
Data protection authorities set the privacy rules.

63
00:04:48,610 --> 00:04:54,448
On the supply side sit NUCTECH's R&D, manufacturing and quality teams, their component suppliers,

64
00:04:54,448 --> 00:05:01,881
local distributors and system integrators, and the field service engineers and trainers who keep the device running.

65
00:05:01,881 --> 00:05:07,508
And around everyone is the public, whose trust depends on screening being effective, safe and fair.

66
00:05:09,158 --> 00:05:14,422
Let's follow one passenger through the full flow, and watch what the officer sees at each step.

67
00:05:14,422 --> 00:05:15,817
Step one: preparation.

68
00:05:15,817 --> 00:05:22,346
The passenger divests: wallet, phone, keys and bulky outer layers go into a tray for the X-ray machine.

69
00:05:22,346 --> 00:05:25,902
Signage and an officer guide them to the scanner.

70
00:05:25,902 --> 00:05:26,793
Step two: entry.

71
00:05:26,793 --> 00:05:30,746
The passenger steps into the channel and stands on the footprint marks.

72
00:05:30,746 --> 00:05:34,928
If fitted, the foot metal detector is already checking their shoes.

73
00:05:34,928 --> 00:05:36,116
Step three: the scan.

74
00:05:36,116 --> 00:05:39,626
The passenger holds their arms slightly away from their sides,

75
00:05:39,626 --> 00:05:45,170
with no need to raise them overhead, and stays still for 2 seconds while the arrays sweep.

76
00:05:45,170 --> 00:05:46,621
Step four: processing.

77
00:05:46,621 --> 00:05:53,741
Over the next couple of seconds the system reconstructs the 3D image and the ATR neural network analyzes it.

78
00:05:53,741 --> 00:05:56,564
No human is looking at a body image.

79
00:05:56,564 --> 00:05:57,937
Step five: the result.

80
00:05:57,937 --> 00:06:03,367
If nothing is found, the screen and LED show a clear signal and the passenger walks on.

81
00:06:03,367 --> 00:06:07,062
That's most passengers, in about 4 seconds in total.

82
00:06:07,062 --> 00:06:11,847
If something is found, a box appears on the cartoon figure, say at the left ankle.

83
00:06:11,847 --> 00:06:16,031
The officer pats down that area only, instead of the whole body.

84
00:06:16,031 --> 00:06:17,918
Step six: resolution and exit.

85
00:06:17,918 --> 00:06:21,503
The officer resolves the alarm, perhaps a forgotten item,

86
00:06:21,503 --> 00:06:28,296
perhaps a real threat that is escalated under local procedures, and the passenger collects their belongings.

87
00:06:28,296 --> 00:06:32,231
Results feed the checkpoint's statistics for supervisors.

88
00:06:33,881 --> 00:06:34,818
Where is it used?

89
00:06:34,818 --> 00:06:38,190
Anywhere people must be screened for concealed items.

90
00:06:38,190 --> 00:06:42,778
In civil aviation, at central security checkpoints and transfer screening.

91
00:06:42,778 --> 00:06:44,762
At customs and border crossings.

92
00:06:44,762 --> 00:06:47,444
In train stations and metro systems.

93
00:06:47,444 --> 00:06:54,916
In government buildings and secure agencies, military bases, prisons, courts and hospitals. At large events and venues.

94
00:06:54,916 --> 00:07:02,272
And in loss prevention: factories, warehouses or mints, where the goal is to stop items leaving, not entering.

95
00:07:02,272 --> 00:07:08,262
The site needs about 1.4 by 1.2 m of floor, 2.5 m of height, standard power,

96
00:07:08,262 --> 00:07:14,488
and an indoor climate from −5 to 45 °C with humidity up to 93%, non-condensing.

97
00:07:14,488 --> 00:07:22,483
The compact footprint means it can often sit beside, or replace, an existing metal detector lane.

98
00:07:24,133 --> 00:07:28,095
When does each stakeholder get involved? Think of it as a lifecycle.

99
00:07:28,095 --> 00:07:34,731
First, need and planning: a threat assessment, a new regulation, or passenger growth creates a requirement.

100
00:07:34,731 --> 00:07:40,949
Then procurement: a tender, technical evaluation, and checks on certification and compliance.

101
00:07:40,949 --> 00:07:45,351
Next, deployment: site survey, delivery, installation and commissioning,

102
00:07:45,351 --> 00:07:49,041
followed by acceptance testing and operator training.

103
00:07:49,041 --> 00:07:54,135
Then operation: every day, every shift, peaking at rush hours and holiday seasons,

104
00:07:54,135 --> 00:08:00,113
with routine checks, preventive maintenance, and software and ATR updates over the years.

105
00:08:00,113 --> 00:08:05,868
Finally, upgrade, refurbishment or end-of-life replacement, typically after many years of service.

106
00:08:05,868 --> 00:08:11,738
The device's life-cycle equipment management feature supports tracking it through every stage.

107
00:08:13,388 --> 00:08:19,323
Now zoom out to the value chain: how value is created, from research to a safer checkpoint.

108
00:08:19,323 --> 00:08:24,862
It begins with R&D: millimeter-wave circuit and antenna design, imaging algorithms,

109
00:08:24,862 --> 00:08:30,934
and AI models trained on large labeled datasets of people with and without concealed items,

110
00:08:30,934 --> 00:08:34,254
across many body types and clothing styles.

111
00:08:34,254 --> 00:08:40,421
Then components: millimeter-wave transceiver chips and antenna arrays, high-speed data acquisition,

112
00:08:40,421 --> 00:08:47,226
computing hardware for reconstruction and AI, metal detector coils, touch screen, frame and enclosure.

113
00:08:47,226 --> 00:08:52,108
Manufacturing and quality follow: assembly, calibration of the RF system,

114
00:08:52,108 --> 00:08:57,975
and testing for detection, safety, electromagnetic compatibility and reliability.

115
00:08:57,975 --> 00:09:01,313
Certification turns performance into market access:

116
00:09:01,313 --> 00:09:05,166
ECAC testing, CE conformity, and national approvals.

117
00:09:05,166 --> 00:09:11,630
Go-to-market comes next: direct sales, distributors and integrators, government tenders and financing.

118
00:09:11,630 --> 00:09:15,060
Then deployment and integration, operations and training,

119
00:09:15,060 --> 00:09:21,196
and after-sales service: maintenance contracts, spare parts, remote diagnostics and software upgrades.

120
00:09:21,196 --> 00:09:25,798
That is where much of the long-term revenue and customer value lives.

121
00:09:25,798 --> 00:09:27,772
Value flows back the other way.

122
00:09:27,772 --> 00:09:33,122
Passengers get speed and dignity, operators get throughput and consistent detection,

123
00:09:33,122 --> 00:09:37,548
regulators get compliance, and society gets safer public spaces.

124
00:09:39,198 --> 00:09:43,268
What know-how does it take to build and run this? Picture a stack of layers.

125
00:09:43,268 --> 00:09:44,158
At the bottom:

126
00:09:44,158 --> 00:09:48,095
RF and millimeter-wave hardware engineering, meaning antennas,

127
00:09:48,095 --> 00:09:52,356
transceivers, calibration and electromagnetic compatibility.

128
00:09:52,356 --> 00:09:59,456
Above that: signal processing and 3D holographic reconstruction, fast enough to finish in about two seconds.

129
00:09:59,456 --> 00:10:03,834
Then AI: data collection, labeling, model training and validation,

130
00:10:03,834 --> 00:10:07,733
to maximize detection while minimizing false alarms.

131
00:10:07,733 --> 00:10:11,480
Then software and user experience: the touch-screen interface,

132
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:16,523
the privacy-preserving cartoon display, device management and cybersecurity.

133
00:10:16,523 --> 00:10:24,013
Then systems engineering: mechanical design, thermal and acoustic control, reliability and manufacturability.

134
00:10:24,013 --> 00:10:28,483
And at the top, operational and regulatory know-how: checkpoint design,

135
00:10:28,483 --> 00:10:33,583
staffing models, officer training, certification strategy and privacy compliance.

136
00:10:33,583 --> 00:10:37,899
A great scanner in a badly designed lane still performs poorly.

137
00:10:39,549 --> 00:10:40,929
How is success measured?

138
00:10:40,929 --> 00:10:43,967
A few key performance indicators matter most.

139
00:10:43,967 --> 00:10:47,920
Probability of detection: how reliably real threats are found.

140
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:55,766
False alarm rate: how often innocent passengers trigger an alarm, which drives pat-downs, staff workload and queues.

141
00:10:55,766 --> 00:11:01,595
Throughput: with a 4-second inspection, the theoretical ceiling is about 900 people an hour.

142
00:11:01,595 --> 00:11:08,824
In practice, divesting and alarm resolution set the pace, and third-party listings cite around 400 an hour.

143
00:11:08,824 --> 00:11:13,127
Availability: uptime, mean time between failures, and repair time.

144
00:11:13,127 --> 00:11:17,495
And total cost of ownership: purchase, installation, 1 kW of power,

145
00:11:17,495 --> 00:11:22,052
service contracts, and, the biggest line item over time, staff.

146
00:11:22,052 --> 00:11:27,376
ATR and the cartoon display move these numbers directly: fewer full-body pat-downs,

147
00:11:27,376 --> 00:11:31,896
fewer officers per lane, and consistent decisions on every shift.

148
00:11:33,546 --> 00:11:35,895
How does it compare with the alternatives?

149
00:11:35,895 --> 00:11:40,309
Walk-through metal detectors are very fast and cheap, but metal only.

150
00:11:40,309 --> 00:11:43,252
Pat-downs are flexible but slow and intrusive.

151
00:11:43,252 --> 00:11:48,689
Backscatter X-ray images well, but uses ionizing radiation and raises privacy issues.

152
00:11:48,689 --> 00:11:55,600
Passive millimeter-wave or terahertz cameras work at a distance, but generally with lower resolution.

153
00:11:55,600 --> 00:12:04,580
Active millimeter-wave portals like the MW1000AA offer the best balance today: detection of metallic and non-metallic threats,

154
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:09,877
automatic recognition, no ionizing radiation, and privacy by design.

155
00:12:09,877 --> 00:12:15,701
It competes in a global market with active millimeter-wave scanners from companies such as Leidos,

156
00:12:15,701 --> 00:12:17,900
Rohde & Schwarz and Smiths Detection.

157
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:21,407
Buyers compare certification, false alarm rate, throughput,

158
00:12:21,407 --> 00:12:25,620
footprint, cost of ownership, service coverage and supplier risk.

159
00:12:27,270 --> 00:12:31,170
Finally, the considerations every buyer and operator should weigh.

160
00:12:31,170 --> 00:12:36,658
False alarms from clothing folds, sweat, or items like belts and buttons add workload.

161
00:12:36,658 --> 00:12:39,529
Passengers with prosthetics, medical devices,

162
00:12:39,529 --> 00:12:45,595
or religious and cultural garments need respectful, well-trained alternative procedures.

163
00:12:45,595 --> 00:12:52,225
Privacy and data governance: decide which images, if any, are stored, for how long, and who can access them.

164
00:12:52,225 --> 00:12:55,560
Cybersecurity matters for any networked device.

165
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:58,011
And supplier and geopolitical risk.

166
00:12:58,011 --> 00:13:04,526
NUCTECH, founded in 1997 from Tsinghua University, has been on the US Entity List since 2020,

167
00:13:04,526 --> 00:13:09,850
and has faced EU scrutiny under the Foreign Subsidies Regulation since 2024.

168
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:15,664
Procurement teams should check the rules that apply in their own jurisdiction.

169
00:13:17,314 --> 00:13:18,624
Let's recap.

170
00:13:18,624 --> 00:13:23,836
Why: to find metallic and non-metallic threats under clothing, safely and quickly.

171
00:13:23,836 --> 00:13:28,985
What: an active millimeter-wave walk-in portal with automatic threat recognition.

172
00:13:28,985 --> 00:13:35,538
How: wide-band holographic 3D imaging plus deep learning, shown on a privacy-protecting cartoon.

173
00:13:35,538 --> 00:13:40,602
Who: passengers, officers, managers, owners, regulators and the supply chain.

174
00:13:40,602 --> 00:13:46,061
Where: airports, borders, transit, government, prisons, events and loss prevention.

175
00:13:46,061 --> 00:13:50,654
When: a 2-second scan inside a lifecycle that spans many years.

176
00:13:50,654 --> 00:13:55,209
That's the NUCTECH™ MW1000AA ecosystem, end to end.
